televised musical performances bright  red decorations and big meals with  relatives  these are common lunar new year  traditions in china but this year chi  ching is missing out on her annual  family gathering in the northeast  the hotel restaurant manager has been  forced to stay put in beijing where she  works because of coronavirus  restrictions  i feel sad that i can't go home this  year and meet my family but i need to  follow the government's instructions  because we're in a pandemic i don't want  to cause trouble for my country my  employer or my family  chinese authorities are battling to  contain an outbreak of the omicron and  delta variants of the virus which has  spread to 20 provinces and 50 cities  including the capital  to control infections many of the  millions of migrant workers who usually  return home for the holiday have been  warned against travel restrictions vary  per region  testing is compulsory in some areas a  period of quarantine or isolation is  required  this video of a communist party official  warning anyone who didn't comply with  rules would be detained went viral last  week  the controls have sparked complaints on  social media with many users saying  migrant workers deserve to be reunited  with family at least once a year  analysts say they're especially impacted  by covert regulations  a lot of workers  actually they have missed the chance to  reunite with their families for  already two years now and a lot of them  can only  connect with their kids  using the phones and they have not  meet up with their parents and they can  only rely on the phone for quite a long  time  authorities are under pressure to keep  infections to zero ahead of the winter  olympics which opens on friday  in beijing decorations are up but  celebrations are muted and large  gatherings banned  before the pandemic hundreds of  thousands of people would flock here to  ditan park to celebrate the lunar new  year there would be dragon dances market  stalls 